const e='<h2>Where the import lives</h2>\n\n  <p>Two routes to the same screen. In the browser it\'s <strong>⋯ › Import Bookmarks &amp; Passwords</strong>. In Settings it\'s <strong>Browser › Import Bookmarks &amp; Passwords</strong>.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/settings-import.webp" alt="The Import Bookmarks and Passwords screen, with buttons for Safari, Chrome, Firefox and Edge." width="1600" height="1077" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Pick the browser you\'re coming from and follow its steps.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Each button walks you through exporting from that browser, then reads the file back in. The shape is the same everywhere: <em>export on the device where your data lives → get the file to your iPhone → open it in XeOS</em>.</p>\n\n  <div class="note">\n    <p><strong>Where your data goes.</strong> Everything imported stays on the device. Passwords are encrypted with Apple\'s own cryptography, with the key held in the iOS Keychain. Nothing is uploaded to XeOS or anywhere else.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>From Safari, and the Apple Passwords app</h2>\n\n  <p>If your logins live in iCloud Keychain or the Apple Passwords app, this is your route. Choose <strong>Import from Safari</strong>.</p>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>On a Mac, open Safari and choose <em>File › Export › Bookmarks</em> for bookmarks, and export passwords from the Passwords app (or Safari\'s Passwords settings) as a CSV.</li>\n    <li>On an iPhone or iPad alone, open the <strong>Passwords</strong> app and use its own export option.</li>\n    <li>Get the exported file onto your device by AirDrop, iCloud Drive, or email to yourself.</li>\n    <li>In XeOS, choose <strong>Import from Safari</strong> and pick the file.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h2>From Chrome</h2>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>On a computer, open Chrome and go to <code>chrome://password-manager/settings</code>. Choose <em>Export passwords</em>. Chrome asks you to confirm with your device password, then saves a CSV.</li>\n    <li>For bookmarks, open <em>Bookmarks › Bookmark manager</em>, then the ⋮ menu and <em>Export bookmarks</em>, which saves an HTML file.</li>\n    <li>Transfer both files to your iPhone or iPad.</li>\n    <li>In XeOS, choose <strong>Import from Chrome</strong> and select each file.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h2>From Firefox</h2>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>In Firefox, open <em>Passwords</em> from the menu, then the ⋯ menu and <em>Export Logins</em> to save a CSV.</li>\n    <li>For bookmarks, open the Library, then <em>Import and Backup › Export Bookmarks to HTML</em>.</li>\n    <li>Transfer both to your device.</li>\n    <li>Choose <strong>Import from Firefox</strong> in XeOS.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h2>From Edge</h2>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>In Edge, open <em>Settings › Profiles › Passwords</em>, then the ⋯ menu and <em>Export passwords</em>.</li>\n    <li>For bookmarks, open <em>Favourites</em>, then the ⋯ menu and <em>Export favourites</em>.</li>\n    <li>Transfer both to your device.</li>\n    <li>Choose <strong>Import from Edge</strong> in XeOS.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <div class="note">\n    <p><strong>Delete the exported files afterwards.</strong> An exported password CSV is plain text. That\'s true of every browser\'s export, not a quirk of this one. Once the import has finished, delete the file from your device and from whatever you transferred it through.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>What you get afterwards</h2>\n\n  <h3>Bookmarks, with folders intact</h3>\n\n  <p>Imported bookmarks keep their folder structure. They show up in the bookmarks bar under the address bar (Cmd Shift B toggles it) and in Settings › Browser › Bookmarks, where you can rename things, drag them between folders and create new ones.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/settings-bookmarks.webp" alt="The bookmarks management screen in XeOS Settings." width="1600" height="1077" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Bookmarks management: folders, renaming, reordering.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h3>Passwords, filled in for you</h3>\n\n  <p>Saved logins land in the browser\'s password panel (<strong>⋯ › Passwords</strong>), where you can search them, reveal one, copy it or delete it.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/browser-passwords.webp" alt="The saved passwords panel in the XeOS browser, listing sites with usernames and hidden passwords." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Search, reveal, copy or delete a saved login.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>More usefully, they\'re offered as autofill when you land on a sign-in form.</p>\n\n  <h2>Why this matters more than it sounds</h2>\n\n  <p>Most of what makes an iPhone-on-a-monitor setup feel like a toy rather than a computer is friction at the login screen. Entering a long password with a trackpad, on a screen across the room, is enough to put anyone off the whole idea. Get your logins in and the rest of the guides here stop being aspirational: <a href="/guides/youtube-fullscreen-on-tv">YouTube on the big screen</a>, <a href="/guides/present-without-a-laptop">presenting from Google Slides</a>, <a href="/guides/work-with-google-drive-and-office-files">working in Drive and Sheets</a>.</p>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> turns an iPhone or iPad into a full desktop on any monitor, TV or projector.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Common questions</h2>\n\n    <h3>Are my passwords sent anywhere?</h3>\n    <p>No. They\'re stored encrypted on the device, with the encryption key in the iOS Keychain.</p>\n\n    <h3>I use the Apple Passwords app, not Safari. Which button?</h3>\n    <p>Import from Safari. That\'s the route for Apple\'s password store.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I import bookmarks without importing passwords?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. Separate files, separate steps. Do either, or both.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I import more than once?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. Importing again adds anything new without wiping what\'s already there.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I still use my phone\'s own password picker?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. The system password sheet is available on the phone side too, so you\'re not locked into one or the other.</p>\n  </div>\n';export{e as default};
